Summary of the core concepts I built/validated in Module 1:

1. Test-Driven Development (TDD) Cycle

Red-Green-Refactor: You wrote failing tests first (shouldReturnACashCardWhenDataIsSaved), implemented the minimal code in CashCardController to make them pass, and refactored the hardcoded values into a dynamic database repository.

2. REST Controllers & Annotations

@RestController: Registers the class as a Spring Bean to handle incoming HTTP requests and automatically serializes Java return values to JSON responses.

@RequestMapping("/cashcards"): Establishes the base URI path for all endpoints in the controller.

@GetMapping("/{requestedId}") & @PathVariable: Maps HTTP GET requests with path parameters. Spring converts the path variable directly into a Java Long.

3. Spring Data JDBC & Persistence

CrudRepository<CashCard, Long>: Abstracts SQL database access by providing ready-to-use CRUD operations (findById, save, deleteById) without requiring manual SQL queries.

@Id Annotation: Marks the primary key field inside the CashCard record.

Optional<T> Pattern: Handles database lookups cleanly using isPresent() to return 200 OK or 404 Not Found.

4. Dependency Injection & Configuration

Constructor Injection: Passing CashCardRepository into CashCardController's constructor allows Spring to automatically wire the bean instance at startup.

Database Initialization: schema.sql creates the database table structure, while data.sql populates seed records before tests execute.

5. Integration Testing Tooling

TestRestTemplate: Simulates real HTTP clients (like Postman) hitting a live Spring Boot server running on a random port during tests without throwing unhandled exceptions on 4xx/5xx responses.

JsonPath (DocumentContext): Parses JSON string bodies to assert specific field types and values ($.id, $.amount).